WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Good morning, John! Happy New Year!

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.920
It's a time for reflecting on the year that has passed, and hoping for a good one to come,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.280
but also, as I am incapable of not doing,

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.400
asking why, WHY, is it this way?

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.500
Why January first? And, if it's gonna be January first,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.400
why isn't January first on the solstice?

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.640
Well, I'll give you a hint. This monumental modern institution of our calendar

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.200
and the way that it works and begins

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.480
has to do with three people who you have never heard of.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.400
The Earth travels around the sun in a circle,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.280
and there is no beginning or end to a circle.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.080
You wanna have a beginning of the year, I get that,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.520
and basically we have traditionally just picked one.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.000
And different cultures have picked different dates for different reasons,

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.940
and they turn out to be really interesting and complicated reasons.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.460
Complicated enough that I can only really talk about one

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.400
and I'm gonna pick the one that I'm most familiar with.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.060
So let's go back in time to 46 B.C.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.160
Julius Caesar has just become the first emperor of Rome,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.140
and the calendar is a mess.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.840
It's based on this calendar that's hundreds of years old,

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.700
that started out only having ten months,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.060
so with December being the last month and March being the first month.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.560
And it's a lunar calendar; every month begins with a new moon,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.500
there's a full moon in the middle, and then it ends again with a new moon.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.720
But since the lunar calendar and the solar calendar don't line up,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.180
they had to have some way of getting realigned each year.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.820
And that system, and I'm not kidding here,

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.740
was 51 days of unaffiliated buffer time

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.300
that occurred between December and March

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.840
that just weren't inside of any month.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.880
but in the 700's BC, one of the original kings of Rome,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.560
Numa Pompilius, one of the guys you haven't heard of,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.580
decided this system just wasn't gonna cut it.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.900
so he added two new months to the beginning of the year.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.680
You've heard of them: January and February.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.420
This resulted in a number of months having Latin prefixes

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.380
that no longer aligned with their position in the year,

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.340
which is an immense source of frustration for me.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.120
But that is when January first became the first day of the new year in Rome.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.420
Weirdly though, January first, kept falling on what

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.340
we would consider different days.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.500
Because the behavior of the moon and the behavior of the sun

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.640
kept not having anything to do with each other,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.120
so in order to keep the summer months happening in the summer,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.480
they had to keep messing with the number of days in each month,

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.520
and then they would have to add extra months sometimes...

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.640
...it was SUPER confusing.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.920
Julius Caesar, like any good dictator, wanted to establish some dang ORDER.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.720
He consulted an Alexandrian astronomer named Sosigenes,

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.680
who suggested that they scrap the lunar calendar completely

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.400
in favor of a solar calendar, like the ones that the recently

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.880
conquered Egyptians had.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.420
Sosigenes had calculated that the solar year was 365.25 days,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.420
so he suggested a year of 365 days long,

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.580
with an extra day thrown in every four years during

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.280
the shortest month, February.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:27.620
(Probably sounds familiar to you!)

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.820
Caesar now had the opportunity to establish when January first

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:32.760
would appear on the solar calendar,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.120
and he could have picked the solstice!

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.600
But as one last, final shout out to the lunar calendar,

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:40.420
he put it on the new moon that was closest to the solstice,

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.880
which just happened to be about nine days later.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.820
So, yes, the date of a new moon in 45 B.C.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.460
decided when Januarary first would appear on the solar calendar.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:50.180
Kinda!

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.900
Because the solar year is actually slightly shorter than 365.25 days,

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.180
the drift began again.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.760
By the Middle Ages, people were confused enough

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:00.780
that they were basically celebrating the new year whenever they felt like it.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.740
Until 1582, when Pope Gregory XIII consulted with an Italian astronomer.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.060
Aloysius Lilius, the last guy you haven't heard of,

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.300
proposed to Pope Gregory a solution for these drifting days.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.240
A slight modification of the leap year schedule,

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.960
having only ninety-seven leap years every four hundred years,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.060
instead of a hundred every four hundred years.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.060
Pope Gregory then instituted and mandated this new calendar,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.300
which we called the Gregorian calendar,

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.220
and we still basically use today.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.840
So Numa Pompilius, Sosigenes, Aloysius Lilius, thank you very much

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.940
for your contributions to our world today.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.440
I'm sorry everybody's forgotten about you.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.640
But here we are now, John, celebrating those people

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:38.800
entering the year 2016, our tenth year of vlogging on Youtube.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:39.840
WOOOW.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.460
I am super interested to see people's comments

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.360
on your last video, but I felt like that was extraordinarily unscientific

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.680
and we weren't getting good data, so I've set up an actual survey

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.600
that people can take. There's a link in the description if you wanna answer

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.140
some questions about what we should do in the next year here on Vlogbrothers.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.440
John, thanks for being a great brother, thanks for another great year,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.611
and I'll see you on Tuesday.

